---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../components/ui/Container.astro";
import AisNav from "../../components/ais/AisNav.astro";

const example = "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q142010092";
const tracker =
  "https://www.myshiptracking.com/vessels/usv-elite-unmanned-mmsi-533134283-imo-0";
---

<MainLayout
  title="How to add or update a vessel in Wikidata"
  description="Correct missing or incorrect vessel details with sourced Wikidata statements. Learn how to identify the right ship, add references and qualifiers, and date MMSI and call-sign information."
  image="/og/ais.png"
>
  <Container class="py-12 lg:py-16">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <AisNav class="mb-8" />
      <h1 class="mb-6">How to add or update a vessel in Wikidata</h1>
      <p class="text-lead mb-8">
        Found a missing builder, an incorrect operator, or a vessel described
        only as “Other”? You can contribute sourced vessel details to Wikidata,
        the shared public database used by Open Waters AIS and many other
        projects.
      </p>
      <nav
        aria-label="On this page"
        class="border-line mb-10 rounded-xl border p-5"
      >
        <ol class="grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <li>
            <a href="#what-changes">What an edit can change</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#find-the-vessel">Find the right vessel</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#add-details">Add or correct a fact</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#references">Add a reference</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#identifiers">Add MMSI and call sign</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#after-publishing">After publishing</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
        <h2 id="what-changes">What an edit can change</h2>
        <p>
          A vessel page combines the ship’s own radio reports with documented
          particulars. Wikidata can supply facts such as vessel type, builder,
          operator, year built, dimensions and former names. Check the source
          links under <strong>Particulars</strong> to see where a fact came
          from.
        </p>
        <p>
          Wikidata edits do not change the vessel’s broadcast name, call sign,
          position, speed, destination or numeric AIS type. For example, a
          survey vessel can correctly broadcast AIS type 90, “Other.” A more
          specific Wikidata type describes its role alongside that radio
          classification. Incorrect transponder settings need to be corrected
          aboard the vessel.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open Waters AIS currently imports Wikidata particulars by{" "}
          <strong>IMO number</strong> on a weekly schedule. Adding an MMSI is
          useful for Wikidata, but does not yet make a vessel without an IMO
          appear in our enrichment. Not every Wikidata property is imported, and
          an official registry may take precedence over Wikidata for a
          particular fact.
        </p>

        <h2 id="find-the-vessel">1. Find the right vessel</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Open the <strong>Wikidata</strong> source link on the vessel page,
            if one is present. Otherwise,{" "}
            <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:Search">
              search Wikidata
            </a>{" "}
            for its name and identifiers. Try former names and alternative
            spellings too.
          </li>
          <li>
            Check that the item represents this individual vessel, rather than
            its class, manufacturer, or another ship with the same name. Compare
            its IMO, operator and history. An MMSI can be reassigned, so do not
            use an MMSI match alone as proof of identity.
          </li>
          <li>
            If no item exists, read{" "}
            <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Notability">
              Wikidata’s inclusion criteria
            </a>{" "}
            before{" "}
            <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:NewItem">
              creating an item
            </a>
            . A Wikipedia article is not required, but the vessel must meet
            Wikidata’s criteria.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          For a new item, enter the vessel’s name as the <strong>label</strong>,
          a short description that distinguishes it from namesakes, and
          documented alternative names as <strong>aliases</strong>. An observed
          AIS spelling can be an alias; it does not need to replace the normal
          name. An account makes it easier to follow your contributions and
          discussions.
        </p>

        <h2 id="add-details">2. Add or correct a fact</h2>
        <p>
          Each row under <strong>Statements</strong> is a property and a value.
          Click <strong>edit</strong> beside an existing fact, or{" "}
          <strong>add statement</strong> for a missing one. Search for the
          property by its name or the P-number below, enter the value, add its
          source, and click <strong>publish</strong>.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>What you know</th>
                <th>Property</th>
                <th>What to enter</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Kind of vessel</td>
                <td>instance of (P31)</td>
                <td>A type, such as survey vessel or ferry</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>IMO number</td>
                <td>IMO ship number (P458)</td>
                <td>The documented IMO number</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>MMSI</td>
                <td>MMSI (P587)</td>
                <td>The verified radio identifier</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Call sign</td>
                <td>call sign (P2317)</td>
                <td>The verified call sign</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Builder</td>
                <td>manufacturer (P176)</td>
                <td>The correct company item</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Operator</td>
                <td>operator (P137)</td>
                <td>The correct company item</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Flag</td>
                <td>country of registry (P8047)</td>
                <td>The country of registration</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Company and vessel-type values link to other Wikidata items. Open the
          candidate item and check its identity before selecting it: an
          unrelated company may have exactly the same name. If the correct item
          is missing, leave the fact out until that item can be established. Do
          not invent an IMO number or a service-entry date to fill a gap.
        </p>
        <p>
          A fact that was true in the past is different from an error. For a
          documented former operator or identifier, preserve its history and add
          dates supported by sources. If you cannot resolve a disagreement, use
          the item’s <strong>Discussion</strong> page rather than repeatedly
          replacing another contributor’s edits. See the{" "}
          <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:WikiProject_Ships/Properties/en">
            ship property guide
          </a>{" "}
          for more fields.
        </p>

        <h2 id="references">
          3. Add a reference: where did the fact come from?
        </h2>
        <p>
          Use a vessel registry, the operator’s fleet page, the builder’s
          announcement, or a reputable maritime publication. For radio
          identifiers, a separate AIS tracker can corroborate the vessel name,
          MMSI and call sign. Verify that the source actually supports the
          specific fact.
        </p>
        <p>
          Prefer sources outside Open Waters. Citing our vessel page for a fact
          that we will then import back from Wikidata creates circular sourcing.
          A source for a vessel’s construction may not establish its current
          operator.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Under the statement, click <strong>add reference</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Choose <strong>reference URL (P854)</strong> and paste the
            supporting page’s full address.
          </li>
          <li>
            Within that same reference block, add{" "}
            <strong>retrieved (P813)</strong> and the date you checked the page.
          </li>
          <li>Save the reference and publish any remaining unsaved edits.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          A reference belongs to the statement it supports. Repeat it on another
          statement if it supports that fact too. If citing two separate
          sources, add two reference blocks.{" "}
          <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Help:Sources">
            Wikidata’s reference guide
          </a>{" "}
          covers other source types.
        </p>

        <h2 id="identifiers">4. Add MMSI and call sign, with dates</h2>
        <p>
          An IMO identifies a hull throughout its life. An MMSI identifies a
          radio assignment and can later belong to another vessel; a call sign
          can also change. Record when the identifier is known to apply.{" "}
          <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Property:P587">
            Wikidata recommends point-in-time qualifiers for MMSIs
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Add a statement: <strong>MMSI (P587)</strong>, followed by the
            verified number.
          </li>
          <li>
            On that statement, click <strong>add qualifier</strong>. Choose{" "}
            <strong>point in time (P585)</strong> and enter the date the source
            establishes the identifier, or the date you verified its listing on
            a current source. Use the source’s observation date for a historical
            report.
          </li>
          <li>
            Click <strong>add reference</strong>. Add the source’s{" "}
            <strong>reference URL</strong> and a <strong>retrieved</strong>{" "}
            date, as above.
          </li>
          <li>
            Repeat for <strong>call sign (P2317)</strong>, including its
            qualifier and reference.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Leave <strong>start time</strong> and <strong>end time</strong> unset
          unless a source establishes when the assignment began or ended. A
          tracker’s last-position timestamp does not establish the assignment
          date.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Where it goes</th>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>What it means</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Main statement</td>
                <td>call sign → 9M4417</td>
                <td>The identifier</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Qualifier on that statement</td>
                <td>point in time → 4 November 2026</td>
                <td>As of this date</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Reference block</td>
                <td>reference URL → supporting page</td>
                <td>Where the fact was checked</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Same reference block</td>
                <td>retrieved → 4 November 2026</td>
                <td>When the page was checked</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          This example uses <a href={example}>USV Elite</a>, with MMSI{" "}
          <strong>533134283</strong> and call sign <strong>9M4417</strong>.
          Compare its <a href={tracker}>independent tracker listing</a> with
          other sources before editing. The dates illustrate a check made on 4
          November 2026. Use the dates your own sources support when editing
          another vessel. USV Elite has no IMO number, so this example improves
          its Wikidata record but will not yet update its Open Waters AIS
          particulars.
        </p>

        <h2 id="after-publishing">
          5. Check your edit and allow time for updates
        </h2>
        <p>
          Expand the reference to check its URL and retrieved date. The
          point-in-time qualifier should sit directly under the main statement,
          outside the reference block. If Wikidata warns that point in time is
          not allowed “as reference,” remove it from the reference and add it
          again using <strong>add qualifier</strong> on the statement.
        </p>
        <p>
          A flag suggesting <strong>inception</strong> or{" "}
          <strong>service entry</strong> is a completeness suggestion, not a
          reason to guess a date. Leave unsupported facts unset. The{" "}
          <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Help:Qualifiers">
            qualifier guide
          </a>{" "}
          explains how dates and other context attach to statements.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your edit becomes public on Wikidata when you publish it. Open Waters
          AIS reads a local copy refreshed weekly, so supported changes are not
          immediate. If the vessel has no IMO, the current importer will not
          pick it up yet. If a fact comes from a registry, follow that
          registry’s correction process; changing Wikidata will not override it
          here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/ais/vessels">Return to the vessel map →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>
